"diagnosis": "Fetal MRI at 28 weeks gestation demonstrates findings highly suggestive of a severe skeletal dysplasia. There is marked, symmetric shortening of all long bones (micromelia), with the femurs and humeri measuring well below the 3rd percentile for gestational age. The thorax is severely narrow and bell-shaped, with significant pulmonary compression. The spine shows diffuse platyspondyly but intact alignment. Intracranial evaluation demonstrates a relatively large skull with frontal bossing, but normal brain parenchymal development. Sulcation is normal for 28 weeks, and the ventricular atrium measures 7.0 mm.\n\nFetal abdominal viscera are unremarkable, but polyhydramnios is noted. Impression: Severe skeletal dysplasia, characterized by micromelia, bell-shaped narrow thorax, and frontal bossing, most consistent with a thanatophoric dysplasia or severe achondroplasia."
